{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004254",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches to Crohn's disease by trait_efo and phenotyping_reported/trait_reported. PGS004254 is preferred because it shows substantially stronger reported discrimination in the selected European validation record (AUROC 0.72, 95% CI 0.70–0.73) and also reports a strong effect size (OR 2.18, 95% CI 2.05–2.32), whereas the two snpnet UKB framework siblings (PGS001330 time-to-event; PGS001331 case-control) have only modest PRS-only metrics (PGS-only AUROC ~0.576–0.579; PGS-only R² ~0.006–0.007) and come from a pan-trait UKB framework publication that domain guidance flags as often less externally robust. The main limitation is comparability: PGS004254’s AUROC appears to be a full-model metric (no PGS-only AUROC/R² provided) and uses covariates (age, sex, 10 PCs), so its apparent advantage could be partly covariate-driven; this prevents a high-confidence, high-quality designation based on the visible metadata alone."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005097",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match heart-failure PRS candidates are present. PGS005097 is preferred because it targets heart failure with a clear disease endpoint (phenotyping_reported: prevalent heart failure), uses a modern cross-ancestry genome-wide shrinkage method (PRS-CSx) with large variant coverage (1,274,692), and is developed across many biobanks (BBJ, BioMe, CKB, FinnGen, GBMI, HERMES, MVP, MyCode, UCLA, eMERGE), supporting transportability. It also reports a representative discrimination metric (AUROC=0.72 with CI) with basic covariates (age, sex, PCs), which is more interpretable than candidates that only provide effect sizes or lack performance metrics. However, confidence is not high because several other direct-match models report similar or higher full-model discrimination (e.g., multiple CHF PheCode models with C-index ~0.75) but their PRS-only contribution is unclear (full-model metrics without PRS-only AUC/R2), and some candidates have unknown covariates or different endpoint framing (incident vs prevalent; CHF phecode vs generic HF), making cross-candidate performance comparability limited from the visible metadata."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001255",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches to otosclerosis (trait_efo=otosclerosis). PGS001255 has stronger and more directly PRS-informative validation evidence visible in the record: it reports PRS-only AUROC (0.6319, with CI) and PRS-only R2 (0.01341), plus full-model AUROC (0.6532) with only basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs). In contrast, PGS002046 reports only a partial correlation (partial-r=0.0182) and lacks AUROC/R2, making cross-model performance comparison less clear. A key limitation is that PGS001255 is from a pan-trait UK Biobank framework paper ('across 813 traits') and uses a time-to-event endpoint ('TTE otosclerosis'), both of which reduce confidence versus a disease-focused, standard case-control endpoint; meanwhile PGS002046 is also from a UKB portability/framework context and likewise not clearly disease-focused. Given the available metadata, PGS001255 is best supported but overall evidence remains limited/indirect, so the direct-match outcome is sub-optimal rather than high-quality."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001280",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 7 candidates are direct matches to pulmonary embolism at the concept level, but they differ in endpoint fidelity and evidence quality. PGS001280 is the cleanest phenotype-aligned option: trait_efo is pulmonary embolism and phenotyping_reported is PE (time-to-event), avoiding the explicit PE+DVT composite used by PGS001277/PGS001278 and the broader mapped EFO shown for PGS001279. Within the same snpnet/UKB framework family (the '813 traits' paper), PGS001280 also has strong and well-specified PRS-comparable metrics (PGS AUROC no covariates 0.60765; PGS R2 no covariates 0.01403) and basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs), with a representative European validation record. However, confidence is not high because (i) these snpnet models come from a pan-trait UKB framework study that domain guidance flags as often less externally robust than disease-focused scores, and (ii) other direct-match candidates include disease-focused (PGS003861) and genome-wide shrinkage / meta-PRS methods (PGS004460/PGS004530) but lack PRS-only discrimination metrics and have unknown covariates, making them hard to compare on a like-for-like basis from the visible metadata."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002075","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 5 candidates are direct matches to urolithiasis (either explicitly `trait_efo: urolithiasis` with `phenotyping_reported: Urinary calculus`, or closely aligned kidney/ureter calculus endpoints). However, the evidence is not strong enough to call a single clearly best model with high confidence because most candidates lack PRS-comparable discrimination metrics (AUC/R²) and two are from a different study family with unknown covariates.

PGS002075 is preferred because it has strong endpoint fidelity (Urinary calculus / urolithiasis) and provides the best available PRS-comparable performance signal among the urolithiasis-labeled models: partial correlation 0.0385 (95% CI 0.0246–0.0524), which is higher than the sibling PGS001864 (partial-r 0.0323). In contrast, PGS001250 reports AUCs but comes from a pan-trait sparse UKB framework paper (a known weaker archetype for external generalisation per the provided domain knowledge) and uses a time-to-event endpoint, which is a warning signal when competing direct disease-status endpoints exist. The 2024 nephrolithiasis models (PGS004493/PGS004563) report only modest per-SD ORs (~1.23–1.28) and have unknown covariates with no PRS-only metrics, making them hard to rank above the portability-family urolithiasis scores on the visible evidence.

Main limitation: the chosen model’s performance reporting is limited to partial-r (no AUROC/R²), and the top alternatives differ in study archetype and endpoint type, so the selection remains moderately uncertain."}
